<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Perth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1266">1266</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-norwegian-war">Scottish–Norwegian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Perth of 2 July 1266 ended the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> over the western islands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus VI of Norway</a> ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-iii-of-scotland">Alexander III of Scotland</a> in return for 4,000 marks and an annual payment of 100 marks, while Norway kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>. It ended the Norse Kingdom of the Isles.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man had become Norwegian territory over centuries in which both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> were still forming themselves as coherent states. Norwegian control had been formalised in 1098, when Edgar of Scotland signed the islands over to Magnus III of Norway. In Norwegian terms the islands were the Sudreys, the Southern Isles, and the long contest over them came to a head in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-iii-of-scotland">Alexander III of Scotland</a>.</p><p>The decisive clash was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> in 1263. Largs is often claimed as a great Scottish victory, but the Norwegians held that their forces, led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a>, had not been fully committed to battle and that the result was inconclusive. Haakon planned to renew the campaign the following summer, but he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> during the winter. His successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-vi-of-norway">Magnus the Lawmender</a>, sued for peace, and the treaty was agreed after negotiations held in Perth during 1265 and 1266.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the treaty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> confirmed Norwegian sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> recognised Scottish sovereignty over the disputed islands to the west. The annual payment of 100 marks was made through the decades that followed. It was cancelled only in 1469, as part of the agreement for the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a> and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, who became Queen of Scotland.</p><p>The historian Michael Lynch has compared the importance of the treaty with that of the Treaty of York of 1237. That earlier agreement defined a border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> almost identical to the modern one, while the settlement made at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> three years after Largs fixed the western limits of the Scottish kingdom and left only the northern isles under the Norwegian crown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Perth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Galloway, Moray and the Norse-ruled north and west were brought under royal control only slowly: after the Battle of Largs in 1263, the <b>Treaty of Perth</b> of 1266 transferred the Western Isles and the Isle of Man from Norway to Scotland, and Orkney and Shetland followed in 1468–1469.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In his reign, later remembered as a golden age, Scotland gained the Western Isles and the Isle of Man from Norway by the <b>Treaty of Perth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-13">13 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Largs in 1263, Norway ceded Man and the Isles to the Kingdom of Scotland by the <b>Treaty of Perth</b> in 1266, the year after the last king, Magnus Olafsson, died.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isles">Kingdom of the Isles</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Perth</b> of 1266 Man passed to Scotland, but it changed hands repeatedly in the Anglo-Scottish wars.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
